Fixed data model, Elasticsearch and device module bugs found while expanding the data model (`#48 <https://github.com/sandialabs/PEAT/issues/48>`_):

- Textual event severities (e.g. ``warning``) were sent as ``event.severity``/``host.event.severity``, which are numbers in Elasticsearch, and caused mapping errors. Numeric severities (within the Elasticsearch ``long`` range) are now sent as numbers, and textual or out-of-range ones as ``log.level``.
- ``original`` file contents inside the ``files`` and ``module`` lists were exported in host documents and ``device-data-*.json`` files.
- ``host.files`` and ``host.successful_pulls`` had no explicit Elasticsearch mapping, and the ``hash.*`` hashes of an event's ``original`` text had no mapping in the events index.
- An empty (disabled) log, events, memory or files index name stopped the saving of all Elasticsearch documents to ``ELASTIC_DIR``.
- De-duplication of nested lists (e.g. the services of an interface) after merging two devices was discarded.
- IPv6 addresses in ``related.ip`` crashed ``export()`` and ``elastic()``, and with them ``peat parse``, the pull of the device and its file exports: IP address sorting supported IPv4 only.
- IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses (``::ffff:192.0.2.1``, e.g. dual-stack sockets in Linux ``netstat`` output) failed IP address validation. They are now stored as the IPv4 address, and an invalid line no longer drops the rest of a ``netstat`` socket table.
- Elasticsearch host documents and the pull summary results of ``peat pull`` were generated before the models were annotated, so values derived by annotation (e.g. hashes and ``related.*`` values of firmware, logic, files and certificates set without ``store()``) were missing, while ``device-data-full.json`` had them.
- ``Interface.description`` (an object) was converted to a string by a validator.
- Sorting lists with both timezone-naive and timezone-aware times (e.g. events) crashed.
- Telnet and SEL serial: the passwords of the Sage Telnet login and of the SEL access level commands (``ACC``, ``2AC``, ``CAL``) were written in plain text to ``peat.log``, ``peat.log.jsonl`` (and with it the Elasticsearch logs index), ``telnet.log`` and the ``selascii-protocol-data_*.json`` files. They are now logged and saved as ``<redacted>``.
- SEL: ETH overflow counters (``$$$$``) were reported as ``-1``; ``ESNTP=UNICAST`` was reported as disabled; ``DNPUDP=REQ`` crashed port settings parsing; ``get_historical_events`` could raise ``StopIteration``; the RTAC web password was read from the cached user name, a ``del`` was a no-op, the MainController tasks were assigned twice, a ``regsiter_stop`` typo, and an invalid ECS event category on the RTAC monitor alert; RTAC RS-232 ports had the type ``rs-232`` instead of ``rs_232``; the SEL-451 UTC offset was formatted as ``-08:0``, and an offset that is not a real UTC offset (e.g. ``30`` or ``nan``) aborted the parsing of the pull results or made the device impossible to export; the FTP trace log contained passwords; zlib-compressed ``.CID`` files were decompressed without a size limit (also by the file signature check that ``peat parse`` runs on every input file); files pulled via Telnet, serial or older FTP lost their directory in ``files`` paths and ``logic.file.path`` (e.g. ``/SET_ALL.TXT`` instead of ``/SETTINGS/SET_ALL.TXT``); an ``EDNP`` DNP3 session count above 1 was reported as disabled.
- SIPROTEC: the uptime was 100 times too large (SNMP TimeTicks are hundredths of a second); the bad-community counters were read from the wrong OIDs; ``ifOperStatus`` values were mapped incorrectly; the system name was cut off when it had no space.
- M340: a ``deepcopy`` crash and unreachable service code; ``module_<slot>`` data was overwritten; a short LED row dropped all SNMP data; ``ifTableLastChange`` was reported as the uptime; the firmware version was duplicated; parsing SNMP and NTP settings from the project file crashed on bytes; the Port 502 (Modbus/TCP) service status and the SMTP server link status were read as text and always decoded as ``no configuration`` and ``unreachable``.
- Sage: every route was a copy of the last route; memory conversion stopped at the first bad task; the USERLOG dataset and action were never set; ``ethernet.xml`` entries were overwritten; ``ACCESS.XML`` passwords were copied into ``dev.extra``, ``parsed-config.json`` and the temporary device info file, and ``ethernet.xml`` passwords were stored; members of a configuration archive could be extracted outside the output directory (``../`` paths); the private keys (``server.key``, IKE ``priv0.key``) and the VxWorks boot line password (``pw``) were copied into ``parsed-config.json``, the temporary device info file, ``dev.extra``, ``firmware.extra`` and Elasticsearch: only their presence is now recorded (``server_private_key_present``, ``ike.privkey_present``, boot line ``password_set``) and ``pw=`` is redacted in the raw boot line; raw archive files are no longer extracted on Python versions without the safe tar extraction filter (before 3.11.4), and extraction failures are logged as warnings; the passwords and password hashes of the VxWorks startup scripts (``loginUserAdd``, ``iam``, ``remCurIdSet``, ``pw=``) and the pre-shared keys and other secret settings of ``ike.cfg`` were copied into ``dev.extra`` (``vxworks_startup_script``), ``parsed-config.json`` and the temporary device info file: they are now ``<redacted>``, the rest of the scripts is kept.
- GE UR relays: pages were downloaded twice; the type of ``lo0`` was not ``loopback``.
- FortiGate: SNMP manager hosts were never captured; NIC counters, speed and link state were lost with the documented ``Rx_Packets=`` output format of ``diagnose hardware deviceinfo nic``; events without a ``level`` crashed parsing; an unknown NIC ``State``/``Link`` value aborted the debug log parse; secret interface settings (e.g. the PPPoE ``password``, also in nested ``config`` blocks such as the ``l2tp-client-settings`` password or the ``wifi-networks`` passphrase and key) were copied into ``Interface.extra``, and with it into the exported device data and Elasticsearch. Encrypted values (``ENC ...``) are now also dropped, whatever the setting is named (also from the ``extra`` of admin users). The ``parsed_config_<name>.json`` (``peat parse``) and ``parsed_sys_config.json`` (SSH pull) files had all the secrets of the configuration (``ENC ...`` passwords, pre-shared keys, SNMP community names): they are now filtered the same way, and keep the other settings.
- ControlLogix: ``ifLastChange`` was used as the interface uptime and crashed when missing; FTP pulls tried to download directories; file names with ``../`` from the device's FTP listing were written outside the output directory. L5X: the IP addresses of remote modules were copied onto the controller.
- iDirect used the Sage options (``options["sage"]``); Linux and BusyBox ``ifconfig`` output crashed the ``ifconfig`` parser; ``ifconfig`` interfaces without the ``UP`` flag were not reported as disabled. SNMP community strings were written to trace logs. The password field of ``/etc/passwd`` (a password hash, unless it is ``x``) was copied into ``dev.extra``, ``parsed/passwd.json`` and Elasticsearch, and lines that could not be parsed were logged: only ``password_in_shadow``, ``password_set`` and ``password_empty`` are now stored, and only the line number is logged. SCEPTRE hostnames of connection endpoints were added to ``related.ip``.
- Camlin Totus: the Wi-Fi hotspot password was stored in ``dev.extra`` and the JSON side files. It and other password, PSK, key, token, community and secret values, and PEM private keys and OpenVPN static keys, are now redacted, including values that are numbers, lists or objects and plural, numbered or ``_string``/``_hash`` key names (e.g. ``passwords``, ``password1``, ``communityString``). The web login password was logged at the ``TRACE`` level with ``-V``.
- easyGen serial pulls never ran (interface type mismatch); OpenPLC task timing statistics in the new runtime format were not parsed; Windows CE ``HKEY_USERS`` was never saved (``HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT`` was copied twice); MicroNet file paths (doubled file names, wrong local paths) and a crash when the logs had no GAP event.
- File names and paths reported by a device (``DeviceData.write_file()``, FTP and SFTP downloads) with ``..`` components or a leading ``/`` could put files outside the output directory. They are now kept inside it.
- Device IDs made only of dots (e.g. a device or IED named ``..``), or with a ``\`` or a Windows drive prefix (``d:``), could put the device's output directory and files outside the ``devices`` directory.
- Windows: device output directories and device file names with characters or names that are invalid on Windows (e.g. the ``:`` of IPv6 and MAC addresses, ``?``, ``*``, ``CON``) could not be written. They are now replaced on Windows only.
